## 1. Introduction
Reactivity is a fundamental chemical property of molecules. Molecules can undergo various types of reactions in all the three states of matter, however, reaction in the solid state differs from other two phases in terms of molecular dispositions and freedom of movement. The reactive functional groups in the solids are locked in the crystal lattice and hence the molecular movements are restricted.1 On the other hand, the reactants in solution or gas phases have more freedom of molecular movements which can bring the reacting functional groups closer, and the reactions take place homogeneously. Because of this restricted movements and non-alignment of reactive functional groups, the reactions in the solid state are in general very sluggish.
On the contrary, molecules are highly oriented in the solid state, specifically in crystals, unlike those in the fluid state. As a consequence, while reactivity in solution is largely a molecular property, solid-state reactivity is characteristic of periodic assemblies of molecules in crystals.2 If the reactive functional groups are already close and in the correct orientation, then reaction can be facilitated in the solid state with ease. This will lead to regioselective and stereospecific products which may otherwise be inaccessible through solution synthesis. The question here is how to bring the functional groups in the molecules closely in correct alignment for the reaction to occur in the solid state? The answer goes back to the classical work of Gerhard Schmidt in the 1960s who established the link between the structure and reactivity in solid-state structures, thereby establishing the fields of solid-state organic photochemistry and crystal engineering.3 Now it is possible to use crystal engineering principles to design reactive solids and conduct reactions in the solid state with greater ease than ever before.4
Solid-state reactions are nothing new and a number of organic reactions can be carried out cleanly in the solid state. However, the mechanisms involved in these reactions are quite complex and may involve the vapour phase,2,5 and mostly bimolecular in nature. Solid-state structural transformations of coordination polymers involve making and breaking of new coordination and/or covalent bonds in more than one direction.
Single-crystal X-ray crystallography is the principal method of determining the three-dimensional structures unequivocally before and after the structural transformation. Therefore, single-crystal to single-crystal (SCSC) transformations are highly desirable to follow the structural changes. However, in the absence of single crystals, the modern technique for determining the structures from powder diffraction patterns has become accessible in recent times and will be a routine technique in the near future. Other spectroscopic and analytical tools such as NMR spectroscopy, UV-Vis and photoluminescence are also used to follow the transformations.
The terms ‘coordination polymer (CP)’, ‘metal–organic framework (MOF)’, ‘metal organic material (MOM)’ etc. have been heavily used to describe various special properties of coordination polymers. Although, there are subtle differences between these terms, they are not treated separately in this review. Therefore, we stick to the term ‘coordination polymer’ (CP), which is more general, to cover all these terms.6,7
This tutorial review is treated as an update to our previous review article8 and hence we have mainly focussed on the scientific papers published after 2007 with some exceptions. All the published papers on this subject could not be discussed due to the format of this tutorial review.
The stimuli for the structural transformations are generally heat, light, mechanochemical force, etc. The effects of such actions are manifested in terms of new bond formation or breakage which results in a change of coordination number and geometry, dimensionality, chirality, interpenetration, etc. In this review we have divided structural transformations based on the cause and effects into three general categories: influence of guest molecules (Sections 2–4) and external stimuli (Sections 5–8), and structural transformations by exchanging the nodes and spacers (Sections 9–12). Finally Kitagawa’s classification, especially, the dynamic behaviour of porous CPs $\left(\mathrm{PCPS}\right)^{9,10}$ has been presented briefly.
## 2. Structural transformation upon removal/uptake of solvents
Single crystals are quite often retained when solvent guest molecules are removed or exchanged with other solvents. Coordination polymeric compounds often exhibit such rigidity and robustness during the solvent removal or the exchange of guest molecules and anions. This type of system is not the main focus of this review, however, a few examples are discussed briefly where they are accompanied by change in the structures and properties.
On the contrary, the removal of coordinated solvents from the CPs often leads to structural rearrangements. In many cases, this is achieved by exposing the compounds in air. This can occur in two ways. If the coordination geometry around metal centre changes but not the coordination number, the structural transformation takes place due to the bonding of the unused donor atom(s) of the already attached ligand(s) closer to the metal centre. In other cases, the vacant coordination site created by the loss of a coordinated solvent is fulfilled by the unused or free donor site(s) of another ligand(s) of an adjacent CP. This leads to a drastic movement of the molecular fragments leading to structural transformation. The monodentate carboxylate ligand, for example, can easily use its unused oxygen atom for chelation upon removal of a coordinating solvent without changing the coordination number. A terminal ligand such as halide and pseudohalide can also bridge the metal centre to compensate this loss. Such structural transformations are presented in this section based on the dimensionality changes.
## 2.1 0-D to 1-D transformations
Thermal reorganization of cyanide bridging. A reversible structural transformation has been observed when a heterometallic hexanuclear cyanide bridged complex $\begin{array}{r}{[{\bf M}{\bf n}({\bf b p y m})({\bf H}_{2}{\bf O})]_{2}.}\end{array}$ $\mathrm{\small{[Fe(HB(pz)_{3})(C N)_{3}]_{4}}}$ $(\mathrm{bpym}\ =\ 2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyrimidine, $\begin{array}{l}{{\displaystyle{\bf H}{\bf B}{\left({\bf p}{\bf z}\right)}_{3}}^{-}}&{=}\end{array}$ hydrotris(1-pyrazolyl)borate) (1) is cooled to $-73\mathrm{~\}^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.^{11}$ The discrete form was found to be stable above this temperature. Rearrangement involves the bridging ${\bf M n}\big(\mathrm{II}\big)-{\bf N C}$ coordination bonds where one ${\bf M n}\big(\mathrm{II}\big)-{\bf N C}$ coordination bond within the cluster was broken and another ${\bf M n}\big(\mathrm{II}\big)-{\bf N C}$ coordination bond was formed between the neighbouring complexes, to result in the formation of a 1-D CP, as shown in Fig. 1 above. During this process, the coordinated water at $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}(\mathrm{II})$ also rearranged and formed new hydrogen bonding. This transformation took place simply with the change in temperature without any loss of water molecules.
The role of cyanide ligands on crystal symmetry and magnetic behaviour. A reversible 0-D to 1-D structural transformation due to the removal of solvents at higher temperature was manifested by the change in the handedness of the crystal from centrosymmetric $\left(P2_{1}/c\right)$ to chiral space group $C2$ as well as the magnetic behaviour from antiferromagnetic to ferromagnetic.12
In the hexanuclear coordination complex $\mathrm{\small{[\{Fe^{III}(T p)(C N)_{3}\}_{4}\cdot}}$ $\{\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{MeCN})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\}_{2}]{\cdot}10\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{\cdot}2\mathrm{MeCN}$ (2), $\begin{array}{r l}{[\mathrm{Tp}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ hydrotris(pyrazolyl)borate) the Tp and $\mathbf{CN}^{-}$ ligands coordinated only to $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ centre to generate $\mathrm{[Fe(Tp)(CN)_{3}]^{-}}$ anions. Two out of four such anions in the hexanuclear unit are coordinated to $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centres in bidentate fashion via two cyanide bridging ligands forming an $\mathrm{[Fe^{III}}_{2}\mathrm{Fe^{II}}_{2}\mathrm{(CN)}_{4}]^{6+}$ square unit. The other two $\mathrm{[Fe(Tp)(CN)_{3}]^{-}}$ anions are coordinated to the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centres in monodentate fashion via one $\mathbf{CN}^{-}$ bridging ligand as shown in Fig. 2. In addition to the three bridging $\mathbf{CN}^{-}$ ligands, each of the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centres are further coordinated by one acetonitrile and two trans-aqua-ligands in a distorted octahedral geometry. Upon removal of some coordinated and lattice solvents by heating single crystals of 2 at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the vacant coordination sites at $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centres have been replenished by $\mathbf{CN}^{-}$ ligands at $\mathrm{[Fe(Tp)(CN)_{3}]^{-}}$ from a neighbouring cluster unit resulting in $[\{\mathrm{Fe^{III}(T p)(C N)_{3}}\}_{4}\mathrm{Fe^{II}(H_{2}O)_{2}F e^{I I}]}$ (3) in an SCSC manner. The intercluster distance between $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , which were linked during rearrangement, changed from 9.96 to $5.16\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ during the structural transformation. In the resultant structure, half of the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ are tetrahedrally coordinated by four $\mathbf{CN}^{-}$ ligands and other half are octahedrally coordinated by four $\mathbf{CN}^{-}$ ligands and two additional cis-aqua-ligands (labelled as Fe4 and Fe3, respectively, in Fig. 2). Three $\mathbf{CN}^{-}$ ligands bonded to $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ are different, one monodentate, the second one bridging tetrahedral $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and the third bridged to octahedral $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centres. In addition, the 1-D chain lacked a centre of symmetry which imposed chirality in 3. The observed antiferromagnetic interaction in 2 was changed to ferromagnetic in 3. This transformation was found to be reversible in the presence of aqueous acetonitrile vapour.
## 2.2 0-D to 1-D to 2-D transformations
Influence of anions and guest molecules. An interesting example for the change in dimensionality was illustrated in a discrete coordination complex $\mathrm{\small{[Co(bpy)_{2}(C H_{3}C N)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]{\cdot(O T f)_{2}}}}$ (4) which was converted to a 1-D polymeric chain of $[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{bpy})\cdot$ - $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathrm{OTf})_{2}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{2}\big]\cdot}\end{array}$ bpy (5) and then finally transformed to a 2-D square-grid sheet of $[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{bpy})_{2}(\mathrm{OTf})_{2}]$ (6).13 The orange single crystals of discrete complex 4, while kept in humid air, spontaneously lost the coordinated $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ accompanied by a drastic structural transformation to a pale pink powder. The coordination bond of one monodentate bpy ligand was broken to become a guest molecule while the other monodentate bpy formed a new coordination bond bridging two Co centres. In order for this transformation to occur, OTf anions were required to move by $3.3\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ and the bpy ligands had to undergo dynamic rotation maintaining orthogonal orientation to form 1-D polymeric chains. The nitrogen atoms of one of the bpy ligands which was hydrogen bonded to the aqua ligand, now bridged $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ centres to form 1-D chains of 5. In the resultant structure determined from the powder XRD data, the guest bpy molecules have been found to be hydrogen bonded to the hydrogen atoms of the aqua ligands of the 1-D chains (Fig. 3). The 1-D chain further undergoes transformation upon dehydration at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The 1-D chains rotated along its axis to fit the coordination sphere of $\mathbf{Co}(\mathrm{II})$ so that the free bpy could coordinate two $\mathrm{Co(n)}$ at both ends resulting in a (4,4) grid structure (6). Therefore, the structural transformation occurs via a dynamic process involving cooperative movements of the ligands and anions. This 1-D to 2-D structural transformation is very similar to that previously reported in $\left[\mathrm{Co}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{bpy})(\mathrm{NCS})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\right]$ bpy by Jacobson et al.14
## 2.3 0-D to 2-D transformation
Change in the bonding modes of chloride and 1,4-dioxane. A $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega)}$ complex $\mathrm{\small{[CuCl_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-}d i o x a n e)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]}}$ (7) upon heating at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , loses the coordinated water molecules and 1,4-dioxane ligands partially to furnish the compound $\left[\mathrm{Cu_{3}C l_{6}}(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{dioxane})_{2}\right]$ (8) in an SCSC fashion. In the resultant structure, each $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ is bridged by four chloride ions to form a 1-D CP comprising $\mathrm{{Cu}}(\upmu{-}\mathrm{{Cl}})_{2}\mathrm{{Cu}}$ rings. In this chain, two consecutive $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ sites are occupied by one and the next site by two dioxanes such that they have distorted square-pyramidal and octahedral geometries, respectively. The bridging by dioxanes across the $\mathrm{\mathbf{Cu}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{4}}$ polymeric chains yielded a 2-D structure as shown in Fig. 4. The effect of this 0-D to 2-D structural rearrangement was further manifested by the observed change in magnetic properties.15
## 2.4 0-D to 3-D transformation
Condensation by dehydration. The complex $\mathbf{\dot{C}d(H{\cdot}M e{\cdot}t r z{\cdot}p b a)}_{2}.$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{4}]$ (9) was obtained after 21 days from $\mathbf{Cd}(\mathbf{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and H-Me-trz-pba by diffusion at room temperature, whereas crystals of [Cd(H-Me-trz-pba)2] (10) with 5-fold interpenetrated diamondoid topology were obtained from $\mathbf{Cd(OAc)}_{2}{\cdot}2\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and the protonated ligand under solvothermal conditions. When heated to $310\ {^\circ}\mathbf{C},$ 9 loses all the four aqua ligands and converted to 10 as shown below in Fig. 5 and this transformation was monitored by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD). On the other hand, a 1-D CP $\mathrm{\small{[Cd(Me-3py-trz-pba)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]}\cdot H_{2}O}$ (11) was obtained in a similar reaction with the ligand Me-3py-trz-pba and a 4-fold diamondoid [Cd(Me-3py-trz-pba)2] (12) was obtained by a diffusion method and solvothermal conditions, respectively. The 1-D CP (11) has been successfully converted to 12 by removing the water molecules at $290^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as followed by PXRD techniques.16
## 2.5 1-D to $\mathbf{1-D^{\prime}}$ transformation
The role of coordinated ethanol on the structural changes. In the nonporous CP $[\mathrm{Ag}_{4}(\mathrm{tmp})_{3}\{\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{CF}_{2})_{3}\mathrm{CF}_{3}\}_{4}(\mathrm{EtOH})_{2}]_{n}$ $\mathrm{{\tmp=}}$ tetramethylpyrazine) $(\mathbf{13})^{\mathbf{17}}$ one EtOH molecule is coordinated to a $\mathrm{\bfAg(\mathrm{{I})}}$ , forming an intramolecular hydrogen-bonding interaction with one of the carboxylate ligands. Heating single crystals of this 1-D CP resulted in the loss of EtOH which led to 1-D to $\mathbf{1-D^{\prime}}$ structural transformation in an SCSC manner as shown in Fig. 6. During this process, the $\mathrm{\bfAg-O(H)Et}$ coordination bond breaks and the carboxylate O which had shown a hydrogen-bonding interaction with EtOH forms a new coordination bond with Ag(I). The resolvation by EtOH was found to be reversible but in a nonSCSC manner.
Change of coordination geometry and magnetic properties. The structure of $1{\cdot}\mathbf{D}$ CP $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})_{4}(\mathrm{L})](\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{SCF}_{3})$ (14) $\mathrm{(L}=2,5\mathrm{-}$ $\mathrm{(pyCH_{2}C H_{2}N{=}C H)_{2}}$ -1,4- $\mathrm{(OH)}_{2}\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{py}}=2$ -pyridyl),18 is shown in Fig. 7. This can lose two coordinated DMF molecules on each $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ atoms in a two-step process yielding two different 1-D CPs with interesting magnetic properties as shown in Fig. 7.19 In the first step the apical DMF ligands at the Jahn–Teller active distorted octahedrally coordinated $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega)}$ centres of 14 have been lost to give 15 in an SCSC manner. In this transformation, the coordination sphere around $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ changed to a distorted square pyramid and the $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ centres in a $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ core became inequivalent. In the second step, the structure of the microcrystalline solid which was obtained by heating 15 at $220~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ was solved from PXRD data and it was found that the coordination sphere around $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ was re-established by the $\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{-}$ anions after the loss of the second DMF molecules from the equatorial position.
During this rearrangement, the anions moved by 4.27 and $5.42\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ (for Cu2 and Cu1 in 15 respectively) to occupy the position vacated by the DMF ligands, and the ligand L also rearranged itself so that the triflate anions could occupy apical position. The $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ core in 16 became almost coplanar to the basal plane of the complex which was orthogonal to the basal plane in 14 and 15. These structural transformations were manifested in their observed magnetic susceptibilities. The magnetic properties of 14 and 15 are similar showing an antiferromagnetic intradimer coupling constant of $J=9.8\pm0.1\mathrm{~K~}k_{\mathrm{b}}$ and $18.2\pm$ $0.1\mathrm{~K~}k_{\mathrm{b}}$ , respectively. However, the antiferromagnetic ${\bf{C u}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{-}{\bf{C u}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ interactions in 16, mediated by two bridging oxygen atoms showed an increase of $J$ value to $1050\pm50\mathrm{~K~}k_{\mathrm{b}}$ . This example demonstrates how the structural transformation on desolvation leads to a dramatic impact on their magnetic behaviour.
## 2.6 1-D to 2-D transformation
The role of nitrate on structural changes. Morsali and co-workers reported a 1-D CP $\left[\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(8\mathrm{-Quin})_{2}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{MeOH})\right]$ (17) (8-HQuin $\mathit{\Theta}=\mathit{\Theta}8$ -hydroxyquinoline) to undergo a structural transformation upon removal of methanol resulting in another
CP $\left[\mathrm{Pb}(8\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Quin})(\mathrm{NO}_{3})\right]$ (18) having 2-D structure.20 The 1-D chains in the former CP is made up of tetranuclear building blocks $\left[\mathrm{Pb}_{4}(\upmu\mathrm{-}8\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Quin})_{4}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{2}\right]$ which are further bridged by bidentate bridging nitrate anions. When the MeOH coordinated to the $\mathrm{{\bfPb}(\mathrm{{\bfII})}}$ is removed, the vacant coordination site is occupied by an unused oxygen atom of the nitrate ligand chelated to $\mathrm{\mathbf{Pb}(\mathrm{_{II})}}$ from the neighbouring chain. In addition, the coordination bond between $\scriptstyle|\mathsf{\mu}_{1,1,1}-\mathsf{O}$ of 8-Qiun and $\mathrm{{\mathbf{Pb}}(\mathrm{{\mathbf{\pi}}_{I I})}}$ is also broken and compensated by the third ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf{{O}}}^{\prime}$ of the $\upmu_{1,3^{-}}$ bridging nitrate, as shown in Fig. 8. A coordination number of six for both the $\mathrm{{\mathbf{Pb}}(\mathrm{{\mathbf{n}})}}$ atoms was preserved and they became equivalent in the rearranged CP. The high temperature $(160^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ and long time (3 days) used for desolvation might be the source of energy in the bond breaking and making process required for this transformation. Further, this transformation of the 1-D to 2-D structure as shown below was found to be reversible, as monitored by the PXRD patterns of both the compounds.
Conformational changes of flexible ligand. Another 1-D to 2-D transformation reported by Espallargas and co-workers involves $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ and $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ CPs containing a flexible ligand 1,4-bis(triazol-1-ylmethyl)benzene (btix).21 The isostructural CPs $\big[\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{btix})_{2}\big(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\big)_{2}\big]\cdot\big(\mathbf{NO}_{3}\big)_{2}\cdot2\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ $[\mathbf{M}\ =\ \mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}\ $ , 19 and $\mathbf{M}\ =\ Z\mathbf{n}$ , 20) contains syn-btix ligands bridging metal centres to form 1-D loops. Each hexa-coordinated metal centre is coordinated by atoms from four btix ligands (N) and two water molecules (O). Thermal extrusion of the coordinated and lattice water molecules led to a drastic rearrangement in the structure as depicted in Fig. 9. The non-coordinating nitrate anions moved to occupy the vacant coordination sites created by the aqua ligands (O) and the syn-btix ligands isomerized to adopt an anti-conformation by rotation around the methylene group. Most importantly, several M–N/O coordination bonds were broken and were reformed with other metal centres during this rearrangement, finally to lead a 2-D sheet-like structure. This transformation was a non-SCSC process and not reversible.
## 2.7 1-D to 3-D transformation
Change in colour and magnetic properties. The 1-D to 3-D structural transformation described below has been found to be accompanied by a change from antiferromagnetic to ferromagnetic behaviour due to thermal dehydration. The pink 1-D CP $\left[\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}(1,4{\-}\mathrm{dioxane})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\right]$ (21) has an octahedral coordination sphere similar to the $\mathrm{{Cu}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ complex 7 but with entirely different packing.22 Each aqua-ligand formed hydrogen-bonding interactions with $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ligands from two different chains and the shortest interchain $\mathbf{Co\cdot\cdot\cdotCo}$ distance was found to be 6.13 Å. Upon heating at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the coordinated aqua-ligands were removed and the coordination sphere was re-established by $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ligands from another 1-D chain, resulting in blue 3-D CP $[\mathrm{CoCl_{2}}(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{dioxane})]$ (22) with diamondoid topology (Fig. 10). Each $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ in the dehydrated CP was then coordinated by four bridging $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ligands and two O from two 1,4-dioxane ligands cis to each other, suggesting a drastic structural transformation took place due to movements of 1-D chains, in order to acquire suitable positions for coordination. This 1-D to 3-D transformation was further manifested by their magnetic properties and a visible colour change from red to blue. Detailed magnetic studies indicated antiferromagnetic behaviour for 21, but the isothermal magnetization at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ for 22 displayed ferromagnetic behaviour. The ability of the halide ions to act as terminal and bridging ligands influenced by coordinating solvents such as water is also demonstrated in this system. It may be noted that a similar $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ complex15 behaves differently as discussed in Section 2.3.
## 2.8 2-D to 3-D transformations
Change in the SBU due to hydration of metal ions. The change of dimensionality from 2-D to 3-D due to the drastic change in the secondary building unit (SBU) has also been observed. Du et al. has reported a CP $[\mathrm{Cu}(5\cdot\mathrm{BN})_{2}]$ (23) $\mathbf{\bar{5}-B N=}$ 5-bromonicotinate) with 2-D layered structure constructed from the $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{CCR})_{4}$ paddle-wheel SBU whose axial positions are occupied by pyridyl N atoms. Upon exposure to moist air, a drastic transformation takes place with the collapse of paddlewheel building unit and formation of an entirely different building block (Fig. 11) leading to the formation of a 3-D framework structure. This transformation takes place in an SCSC fashion and with visible colour change from green to blue, due to a change in the coordination geometry from square-pyramidal to octahedral, where two water molecules are bonded to $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ in trans manner.23 The driving force for this interconversion is likely due to the presence of free donor nitrogen atoms closer to the metal ions.
Zipper-like action due to solvent removal. Recently an interpenetrated 2-D CP has been reported by Lin et al. to undergo a 2-D to 3-D structural transformation by exchanging with the solvents. The four-fold interpenetrated 2-D CP $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{2}L(D M F)_{4}]cdot2D M F{\cdot}4H_{2}O}}$ (24) $\mathrm{{[H_{4}L~=~}}$ methanetetra(biphenyl-4-carboxylic acid)], transformed to 3-D CP $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{L}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]$ xsolv (25) in an SCSC manner when the coordinated DMF solvents were exchanged with water in $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ , as shown in Fig. 12.24 The distorted octahedral coordination sphere in $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ was completed by two chelating carboxylates and two coordinated DMF molecules. In the interpenetrated 2-D layers, the $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}]$ groups are arranged very close to each other. When soaked in $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ , the removal of the coordinated DMF molecules triggered movements of these 2-D layers and the carboxylates now bonded the $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ atoms between these two layers, which is responsible for the increased dimensionality. The two $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ centres in the newly formed 3-D porous CP have distorted trigonal-bipyramidal geometry. The $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{5}$ core in $z\mathrm{n1}$ is made up of O atoms of four carboxylates and the Zn2 center picked up two water molecules from the solvent. The overall structure has a chiral diamondoid topology, and the four-fold interpenetration was maintained. Due to these structural transformations, the pore volume increased from $28.8\%$ in 24 to $41.4\%$ in 25, as manifested by a 7-fold enhancement of hydrogen uptake from 0.3 to $1.75\%$ . The BET surface area also increased from 177 to $1170\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm. The $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ analogue also behaves in a similar manner.
## 2.9 3-D to $\mathbf{3-D^{\prime}}$ transformation
Transformation of MOF-5 to MOF69C. The well-known MOF-5 $[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}]^{25}$ (bdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}_{1,4}$ -benzenedicarboxylate) has been found to be sensitive to moisture and thus converts to another MOF compound $\mathrm{[Zn_{3}(O H)_{2}(b d c)_{2}]}$ (MOF-69C) upon exposure to humid air.26,27 In MOF-69C, one third of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ has octahedral coordination and two-thirds of the tetrahedrally coordinated $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ shares edges instead of corners as found in MOF-5 (see Fig. 13). The oxide anion in the $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O^{6+}}$ cluster abstracts a proton from absorbed water molecule to produce a hydroxyl anion. The detailed mechanism of this transformation was, however, not very clear.
Change in helicity upon dehydration. In this example, the removal of aqua ligands influences the change in the helicity of the CP and hence the observed behaviour in the solid state. Chen et al. reported a 3-D chiral CP $\left[\mathbf{Cd}(\upmu\mathbf{-}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})(\mathbf{bct})\right]$ (26) $\mathrm{(H_{2}b c t=}$ bis(carboxymethylmercapto)-1,3,4-thiadiazole acid) with mpm helicities, along the $a\cdot$ , $b\cdot$ - and $c$ -axes, respectively.28 Upon removal of the aqua ligand at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ a drastic structural rearrangement took place that led to the formation of the anhydrous CP [Cd(bct)] (27) containing pmm helices, in an SCSC manner as shown in Fig. 14. In other words, the helicities along the $a\mathrm{-}$ and $b$ -axes have been found to reverse but the helicity along $c$ -axis remained the same. In the absence of a aqua ligand, the distorted octahedral coordination sphere around $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ in 27 has been re-established by one free carboxylate O and one anti-carboxylate O of bct, which were in close vicinity of $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ . The details of these changes are shown in Fig. 14a.
The modes of coordination for carboxylate changed from monodentate to syn–anti bridging and for another case, it changed from syn–anti bridging mode to chelating bridging. Both the racemates 26 mpm and 26 pmp exhibited similar rearrangement to form 27 pmm and 27 mpp. Interestingly, these transformations were found to be irreversible. The bulk, of course, contains equal amounts of mpm and pmp, and the authors found that the pmp helices were also transferred to mpp conformations upon dehydration. The high temperature $(180~^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ used to remove aqua ligand is believed to play a significant role providing energy for the molecular movements during this transformation.
Change in chirality upon desolvation. Zhang and co-workers reported an example of chiral to achiral transformation of diamondoid networks by exchanging the lattice solvent.29 A chiral diamondoid framework in $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{ain})_{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{A}$ (28) $\left(\mathrm{Hain}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{\Lambda}$ 2-aminoisonicotinic acid) was observed to transform to an achiral framework of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{ain})_{2}{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{MeOH}$ (29) maintaining the single crystallinity. It is to be noted that the analogous $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{ain})_{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{DMF}$ (30) is also achiral like 29. The difference in the chirality between the analogous frameworks of 28 and 29 or 30 originates from the relative disposition of unsymmetric ain ligands that resulted in a twisted framework. The two carboxylate groups of ain ligands around distorted tetrahedrally coordinated $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ centres became inequivalent due to an uneven distribution of hydrogen bonding. On the other hand, the ain ligands around $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ became equivalent due to structural rearrangement upon exchange of guest solvents. The ain ligands rotated and arranged themselves to form a regular diamondoid framework, and the monodentate carboxylate group became chelating, resulting a change in the coordination environment around $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ from distorted tetrahedron to distorted octahedron (Fig. 15). As the unsymmetric elements were lifted due to these rearrangements, the chiral frameworks $\left(P2_{1}2_{1}2\right)$ became achiral (Fddd) and this transformation was manifested by improved $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake. It is important to note that an ideal diamondoid framework crystallizes in space group $F d\bar{3}m$ and constitutes an equal number of opposite helices resulting an achiral meso-state. However, twisting the framework often reduces symmetry, resulting in chirality.30
## 2.10 Retention of coordination spheres by migration of guest molecules
Rosseinsky et al. reported a 3-D CP $[\mathrm{Co_{2}(b p y)_{3}(S O_{4})_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}}](\mathrm{bpy)}\cdot$ 一 $\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\right)$ (31) to undergo a reversible ligand substitution reaction upon removal of the coordinated aqua ligand by the guest bpy ligands leading to a structural transformation.31 There are two structurally distinct $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ centres with octahedral coordination spheres in the solid. A two-dimensional square-grid is formed by one $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ whose coordination sphere is composed of four bridging bpy and two trans sulfate anions. The coordination sphere of the second $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ is completed by two bridging bpy, two bridging sulfate and two aqua ligands. The overall structure of this CP is 3-D due to the bridging ${\mathrm{SO_{4}}}^{2-}$ anions to the Co(II) centres as shown in Fig. 16. There is also a free uncoordinated bpy ligand present as a guest which is hydrogen bonded to the aqua ligands, in addition to the lattice MeOH hydrogen bonded to the sulfate anions. When pink single crystals of this complex were heated at $95~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the aqua ligands escape from the crystals and the coordination sphere is re-established by lattice MeOH and bpy guests to give purple single crystals. In order to coordinate to $\operatorname{Co}(\mathfrak{n})$ centres, MeOH and bpy molecules should move by 4.2 and 2.81 Å respectively. The interesting aspect here is that at each $\mathrm{Co(n)}$ centre, both the aqua ligands have been replaced either by two bpy or two MeOH ligands alternatively while the second coordinating site of bpy remains unchanged. In order for these ligand substitutions and rearrangements to occur, a cooperative perpendicular motion of neighbouring bpy molecules takes place on either side of the chains at half of the metal centres. In addition, the increased steric crowding upon coordination of bpy compared with the removal of aqua ligands results in widening the S–O–Co angle to the bridging sulfate from $135.1^{\circ}$ in 31 to $156.3^{\circ}$ in 32, but remaining at $133.9^{\circ}$ at the less crowded methanol-coordinated $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ site. This structural transformation was found to be reversible i.e., the uptake of atmospheric water displaces the coordinated bpy and MeOH to produce the rehydrated phase. This is also an unusual case where highboiling coordinated water molecules are replaced by low-boiling methanol molecules upon heating.
## 2.11 Change of interpenetration by desolvation and heating
In the transformation reported by Chen et al., a reversible framework deformation of $\mathrm{\small{[Ag_{6}C l(a t z)_{4}]O H{\cdot}6H_{2}O}}$ (33) (Hatz $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 3-amino-1,2,4-triazole) has been observed both by cooling and dehydration. During this transformation Ag(I)–ligand coordination bond breakage and reformation take place maintaining the single crystallinity and the degree of interpenetration increased from five- to six-fold.32
The porous interpenetrated network structure in $[(\mathbf{ZnI}_{2})_{3}-$ $\mathrm{\left(TPT\right)}_{2}\mathrm{]}{\cdot}5.5\mathrm{PhNO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (34) $\mathrm{(TPT~=~tris(4{\cdot}p y r i d y l)}.$ triazine) has been reported by Fujita and Kawano that transformed to a noninterpenetrated network through a crystalline-to-amorphousto-crystalline (CAC) pathway upon heating to $200-300\ {^{\circ}}\mathbf{C}$ as shown in Fig. 17.33 The initial crystalline phase (phase I) with doubly interpenetrated structure was found to be stable up to $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and then transformed to an amorphous phase (phase II). With further heating, phase II transforms to a new crystalline phase (phase III) at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ which was stable until decomposition occurred at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . During this transformation, the initial colourless sample (phase I and II) turned light yellow in phase III. The structure determination of phase III from powder diffraction data showed it to be a ‘saddle-type’ structure forming 1-D chains held together by $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interaction between stacked TPT ligands which might be the origin of the light yellow colouration. The most important observation to note is that there is no interpenetration found in phase III. The drastic rearrangement involving cleavage and formation of $\mathrm{zn-N}$ coordination bonds, that takes place during the transformation from an interpenetrated to non-interpenetrated structure, might be the cause for the formation of the amorphous phase. The authors anticipated that the amorphous phase possessed some structural memory effect that enabled it to transform to another crystalline phase upon heating.
Kitagawa called this type of amorphous phases as ‘imperfect crystallinity’ which means only the long-range order of the frameworks is lost.34 The isostructural compound $[(\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2})_{3}-$ $\mathrm{\big(TPT\big)}_{2}\mathrm{]}{\cdot}5\mathrm{PhNO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (35), a kinetic product obtained as a powder by instant mixing of $\mathrm{ZnBr}_{2}$ with the ligand, has also exhibited such CAC transformation, however it differed in the solid-state reactivity yielding another interpenetrated network of $[(\mathrm{ZnBr}_{2})_{3}(\mathrm{TPT})_{2}]$ (36) when heated to $280^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .35 An analogous compound $[(\mathbf{ZnBr}_{2})_{3}-$ $\left(\mathrm{TPT}\right)_{2}\mathrm{]}{\cdot}6\mathrm{PhNO}_{2}$ (37) having non-interpenetrated structure also transformed to 36 via such a CAC transformation at $300^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.$ It is important to note that 35 and 37 yielded the same product despite having different structures. Single crystals of the hightemperature thermodynamic product have also been synthesized using a layer diffusion method.36
Krautscheid et al. obtained a four-fold interpenetrated network structure $\mathrm{\small{[Zn(H\mathrm{-}M e\mathrm{-}t r z\mathrm{-}p b a)_{2}]\cdot4H_{2}O}}$ (38) with diamond topology by a diffusion method while 5-fold interpenetrated $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-Me}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{tr}\mathrm{z}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{pb}\mathrm{a})_{2}]$ (39) was formed in solvothermal conditions by reacting $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ and H-Me-trz-pba (shown in Fig. 18). The CP, 38 has been converted to 39 by heating to ${70}~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.$ The solid 38 undergoes a reversible phase transition to another form whose structure resembles that of 10. The interconversion of all these compounds were corroborated by thermoanalytical techniques and XRPD studies.16
## 3. Higher to lower dimensional transformations
In many structural reversible transformations discussed here or reported in the literature, the dimensionality is reversed due to solvation. However, there are not many examples available only for the ‘depolymerization’ reactions, i.e., transformation from higher-dimensional network structures to lower-dimensional structures. This can be achieved by delinking the metal–spacer connections by solvents or guest molecules. Of these, hydration of CPs is expected to lead to such transformations due to the ubiquitous nature of water in solvents and atmosphere.
## 3.1 2-D to 1-D transformation
A 2-D CP $\mathrm{\small{[Mn_{3}(i p)_{3}(b i p y)_{2}]cdot2H_{2}O}}$ (40) $\left({\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{ip}}=\right.$ isophthalic acid, bipy $\mathbf{\Sigma}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine) was reported to change to a 1-D zigzag chain upon soaking in water for several days. The structure of this $\mathrm{CP\40}$ is composed of trimanganese cluster units held together by six octahedrally coordinated carboxylate O atoms whereas the terminal $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}(\mathrm{II})$ atoms adopt distorted squarepyramidal geometry by three carboxylate O atoms and two $\mathbf{N}$ atoms from chelating bipy ligands. When single crystals of this CP was soaked in water for several days, hydration modified the SBUs of the resultant compound to a 1-D zigzag CP $\mathrm{\small{[Mn(ip)(bipy)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}\cdot H_{2}O}$ (41) in an SCSC fashion as shown in Fig. 19. It is noted that the Mn : bipy ratio changed during the conversion.37
## 3.2 3-D to 2-D transformation
A 3-D porous CP with sodalite topology $[\mathrm{Ag(dpzm)(DMSO)}]\mathrm{-}$ $\mathrm{ClO_{4}{\cdot}0.1D M S O{\cdot}0.9E t O A c}$ (42) $\mathrm{{(dpzm=}}$ di-2-pyrazinylmethane), has been demonstrated recently by Sumby to undergo porous 3-D to nonporous 2-D structural transformation upon desolvation (Fig. 20).38 The removal of lattice solvents and coordinated DMSO from penta-coordinated $\mathrm{\sfAg(\mathrm{I})}$ by submersing in paratone oil led to the collapse of the framework resulting in a 2-D framework of $\mathrm{\big[Ag(dpzm)\big]ClO_{4}{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ (43). The mechanism of such a transformation is not clearly understood. The 3-D PCP contains 1-D channels directed along the $c$ -axis with a honeycomb-like arrangement which also exhibited reversible expansion and contraction (known as breathing, discussed in Section 13) upon exposure to different solvents.
## 4. Structural transformation due to gas–solid reaction
## 3-D to 1-D and 1-D to 0-D transformations
Four different 3-D structures of $[\mathrm{Zn}\{\mathrm{Au}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}\}_{2}]$ (44) with tetrahedral zinc(II) nodes and $\mathrm{Au(CN)}_{2}^{\phantom{-}}$ anion as spacer ligands having quartz and cristobalite network structures with 3- to 6-fold interpenetrations have been assembled by changing the counter ions, solvents and reaction conditions. The solid-state emissions with wavelengths ranging from 390 to $480~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ observed in these compounds can be linearly correlated to the Au Au distance. Of these the $\mathfrak{a}$ and d forms react with $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ vapour in a stepwise manner yielding 1-D CP $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}-$ $\{\mathrm{Au}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}\}_{2}]$ (45) and probably a coordination complex $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{4}]\mathrm{-}$ $[\mathrm{Au}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}]_{2}$ (46). These reactions are reversible to produce the same isomers. The luminescent properties are changed by the Au Au distances due to binding of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ to the $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ centre. An excess of ammonia reacts with 44 to form a white powder 46 which exhibits a single emission peak at $430~\mathrm{{nm}}$ on excitation at $365~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . This white powder changed to a yellow powder of 45 in air and showed a single emission at $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ upon excitation at $400\mathrm{nm}$ . Due to the reversibility of the reaction, fast response to ammonia, colour change from white to yellow under normal light and blue to green under UV light, and detection limit to 1 ppb, these coordination polymers are considered to be ideal ammonia sensors.39
## 5. Influence of heat
## 5.1 1-D to 2-D transformation
The 1-D zigzag CP $[\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-bpy})]$ has two polymorphic modifications $\mathbf{47}\upalpha$ and $\mathbf{47}\upbeta$ . Englert et al. observed that upon cooling to $-143\ {}^{\circ}\mathbf{C},$ both the polymorphs undergo structural transformation to a 2-D CP (48) as shown in Fig. 21.40 The distorted tetrahedral coordination sphere around $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ in 47 is completed by two bridging bpy and two terminal chloride ligands. In the crystal structure the zigzag chains are arranged one below the other. By lowering the temperature, the chains approach toward each other and the metal-centred angles vary to support new $Z\mathrm{n-Cl}$ bond formation through bridging. In the rearranged product, the $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ centres have distorted octahedral geometry coordinated by four bridging chloride and two transbridging bpy ligands. The $Z{\mathrm{n-Zn-Zn}}$ angle in the zigzag polymer changed upon rearrangement from $102.5^{\circ}$ in the $\textsf{\textsf{Q}}$ -form and $102.1^{\circ}$ in the $\upbeta$ -form to $180^{\circ}$ in 48, reflecting cooperative molecular movements, which was found to be reversible by increasing the temperature above $87~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ . This illustrates (Fig. 21) a simple example for topochemical structural transformation that occurred by the change in temperature accompanied by the change in the coordination number and geometry.
## 5.2 0-D to 1-D and 2-D transformation
Solid-state dehydrochlorination. In the metal complexes $[\mathbf{bpy-H_{2}}][\mathbf{MCl_{4}}]$ $[\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Pt}\$ , 49; Co, 50 and Zn, 51), the hydrogen atoms of the bpy- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ are hydrogen bonded to two chlorine atoms of the anions to furnish hydrogen-bonded networks (Fig. 22). On heating these complexes in the solid state, dehydrochlorination occurs leading to the structural transformations. The products, obtained as crystalline powders, have been confirmed by matching their PXRD patterns with already known compounds. In the cases of $\mathrm{{Pt}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ and $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ , linear and zigzag 1-D CPs are formed respectively, while the $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ complex yields a 2-D CP. The structures of $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ and $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ polymers match with 47 and 48, respectively, in the above example, as shown in Fig. 21. This process can be reversed by exposing the CPs to HCl vapour for $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ and $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ .41
## 6. Structural transformation by photochemical reactions
to photochemical reactions. In many cases in the absence of suitable single crystals, photochemical cycloaddition reactions have been proven to be a useful tool to follow such molecular movements and transformations in the solid state. The structural changes observed in CPs during photochemical reactions are also similar to those observed in thermal methods which include, change of coordination number and geometry around a metal centre, change in connectivity, dimensionality, interpenetration and associated changes in the physical and chemical properties.
## 6.1 0-D to 1-D conversion
In solid-state photochemical $\left[2\substack{+}2\right]$ cycloaddition reactions, new $\upsigma$ -bonds form between two carbon atoms at the expense of $\pi$ -bonds.42 Therefore, all these reactions in general can be regarded as structural transformations. Here we shall discuss some interesting examples where unexpected photochemical behaviours have been observed due to structural rearrangement in the system prior to photochemical reactions and, quite commonly, change in the connectivity and dimensionality due
Here is an example described in which a hydrogen-bonded coordination complex undergoes a topochemical $[2+2]$ cycloaddition reaction to form a 1-D CP. The bpe- $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ ligands from adjacent zwitterionic complexes $\left[\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{bpe}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H})_{2}(\mathrm{TFA})_{4}\right]$ $\left(\mathrm{TFA}\ =\ \right.$ $\mathbf{CF}_{3}\mathbf{CCO}_{2}^{-})$ (52) have been assembled as pairs in a face-to-face manner through complementary ${\bf N-H\cdot\cdot\cdot O}$ hydrogen bonds between the protonated imine nitrogen atoms and the oxygen atoms of the monodentate TFA ligand to furnish a zigzag hydrogen-bonded polymer. This compound has been found to produce $100\%$ photodimerized product, yielding a 1-D CP by the transfer of $\mathbf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ proton to TFA anion and formation of $\mathrm{\mathbf{P}b-N}$ bond, as shown in Fig. 23. The gel-like product on recrystallization yielded a slightly different 1-D CP due to crystallization conditions.43
## 6.2 1-D to $\mathbf{1-D^{\prime}}$ transformation
Here Schmidt’s criteria for $\left[2\substack{+}2\right]$ cycloaddition reactions have been used to follow the molecular movements in the solid state due to desolvation. The 1-D polymeric chains of [Cd(bpe)- $(\mathbf{CH}_{3}\mathbf{CO}_{2})_{2}\big(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\big)\big]_{n}$ (53) are aligned parallel due to hydrogenbonding interactions between chains via coordinated aqua-ligands and chelating acetate ligands.44 However, the photoreactive $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ bonds are stacked in criss-cross fashion and are away from each other by $4.33\mathrm{{\mathring{A}}}.$ Upon irradiation under UV light, only $\sim33\%$ photodimerization reaction occurred to form rctttpcb [tetrakis(4-pyridyl)cyclobutane] as determined by ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectroscopy. The dehydrated sample obtained by heating the single crystals, however, undergoes $100\%$ photodimerization reaction. The observed quantitative conversion indicates some structural rearrangement in the dehydration process which led to the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ bonds in the bpe ligands to have parallel orientation with appropriate distance satisfying Schmidt’s criteria. The distance between $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ and the nearest non-bonding acetate oxygen which is hydrogen bonded with the aqua-ligand is $4.44\mathring\mathrm{A}.$ It is likely that this acetate oxygen from the neighbouring polymeric strand upon removal of the aqua ligand migrates to coordinate the $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ centre in bridging mode to form either a ladder-like 1-D CP or a 2-D CP having infinitely arranged bpe ligands. From the observed photoreactivity and $^{113}{\bf C d}$ solid-state NMR spectra, it was anticipated that the resulting dehydrated CP was a ladder-like CP, as shown in Fig. 24.
## 6.3 1-D to 2-D transformation
Drastic molecular movements have also been observed during the cycloaddition reaction of a triple-stranded 1-D CP $[\mathrm{Pb}_{3}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{bpe})_{3}\cdot$ $\left(\mathsf{\Pi}[\mathsf{H}\mathsf{-O}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{F}_{3}]_{2}\big(\mathsf{\Pi}[\mathsf{H}\mathbf{-O}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{H}_{3}]_{2}\big(\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{F}_{3}\big)_{2}\right]$ (54) in the solid state.45 In this structure, the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ bonds of all the three polymeric strands were stacked parallel and separated by $3.93{\mathring{\mathbf{A}}},$ suitable for photodimerization reaction. The shortest distance of $7.61\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ between the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ bonds in the adjacent polymers ruled out any inter-ladder photodimerization to take place. In the first step of photodimerization process, only a pair can react among the three bpe ligands which satisfy Schmidt’s criteria, and will account for $67\%$ of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ bond conversion to cyclobutane rings. This leaves
$33\%$ of unreacted bpe ligands. In the second step, the preorganization of the unreacted bpe ligands occurs by lateral movements of the adjacent polymers during the photodimerization process (Fig. 25). This has been supported by the fact that when single crystals were used to investigate the reactivity, the first process takes place in just $40~\mathrm{min}$ while the second process was very slow. The anisotropic movements of the $67\%$ photodimerized triple strands was found to be hastened by a grinding process.
## 6.4 2-D to 2-D transformation
In the 2-D CP $[\mathrm{Cd}_{2}(\mathrm{pvba})_{2}(\mathrm{tbdc})(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}]$ (55) $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{tb}\mathrm{dc}=2,3,5,6-$ tetrabromobenzene-dicarboxylic acid, $\mathrm{{Hpvba}=\it{t r a n s-2}\mathrm{{(4^{\prime}{-p y r i d y l})\cdot}}}$ vinylbenzoic acid), the $[\mathbf{Cd}_{2}(\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C})_{4}(\mathbf{pyridyl})_{2}]$ core acts as an SBU, connecting the tbdc ligands in one direction, and a pair of pvba ligands which are aligned in head-to-tail parallel fashion in the orthogonal direction, to form a sheet structure. The $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ bonds in these pvba ligand pairs are well aligned to undergo $100\%$ $\left[2\substack{+2}\right]$ cycloaddition reaction in the solid state under UV light, yielding the cyclobutane derivative. This photochemical reaction appears to facilitate structural transformations from one $2{\cdot}\mathbf{D}$ structure to another in the solid state as shown below in Fig. 26. Moreover, on heating, the cyclobutane rings in the photodimerized product can be cleaved in two different ways to yield a mixture of trans- and cis-isomers of pvba quantitatively as monitored by $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectroscopy. This transformation, depicted in Fig. 26, appears to be the first attempt to reverse the cyclized product in coordination polymers.46
## 6.5 2-D to 3-D transformation
Wu et al. reported a 2-D CP $\mathrm{\big[Mn_{2}L_{2}\big(H_{2}O\big)_{2}\big]{\cdot}3H_{2}O}$ (56) $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{L}\right.=$ trans-5-(2-(pyridin-4-yl)vinyl)isophthalic acid), with a bilayer structure which exhibits structural transformation upon photodimerization reaction.47 All the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}(\mathrm{II})$ centres in this CP have distorted octahedral geometry and are coordinated by two chelating carboxylate $^{\mathrm{~\small~O~}}$ atoms, two syn-anti bridging O atoms from two different ligands, one N atom from the fourth ligand and an aqua-ligand. These 2-D bilayers are stacked parallel in an . . .AA. . . fashion. The $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C=C}$ bonds of ligand were found to stack parallel in head-to-tail fashion. However, the centre-to-centre distance between $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ bonds in the bilayer was 4.21 Å which is slightly longer than Schmidt’s limit. On the contrary, they are closer between the adjacent bilayers $(3.63{\mathring{\mathrm{~A}}})$ , suitable for photodimerization reaction as shown in Fig. 27. This CP underwent photodimerization in quantitative yield and in SCSC manner among the interlayer pairs, which led to the 2-D to 3-D structural transformation due to photochemical reaction. It may be noted that the guest and the coordinating water molecules do not lead to destruction of the single crystals during the cyclization reaction.
## 6.6 Interdigitated 2-D to three-fold interpenetrated 3-D transformation
The CP $[\mathrm{Zn_{2}(c c a)_{2}(4\cdot s p y)_{2}}]$ (57) $\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{2}\mathbf{cca}=4$ -carboxycinnamic acid, $\mathbf{4-spy}=4\mathbf{\cdot}$ -styrylpyridine), has an interdigitated sheet-like structure in which paddle-wheel building blocks $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C})_{4}]$ , are formed by the dicarboxylate and the axial positions occupied by the monodentate terminal 4-spy ligands are responsible for the interdigitation of the layers.48 Each window of the (4,4) connected 2-D sheets was interdigitated by four 4-spy ligands from four different sheets above and below the plane. The parallel stacking of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ bonds of 4-spy ligands in head-to-tail fashion occurring between the first and fourth layers are suitable for the $[2+2]$ cycloaddition reaction as shown in Fig. 28. Upon irradiation under UV light, it underwent photodimerization reaction in a SCSC manner resulting in the formation of triply interpenetrated 3-D pillared-layer coordination polymer. Here a pillared-layer three-fold interpenetrated 3-D CP has been assembled from interdigitated 2-D sheets by a photochemical method.
## 7. Influence of mechanochemical grinding
Mechanochemical grinding of reactants with or without solvents is a popular and environmentally friendly green synthetic route for many organic, inorganic, organometallic and metal–organic hybrid materials.49 However, this grinding has also been shown to result in various types of structural transformations due to loss or uptake of solvents, pedal-like motion and movements of molecular fragments due to mechanical force. Here, we restrict ourselves to discuss only structural transformations resulting from mechanochemical grinding. Such transformations by the mechanochemical grinding cannot be investigated directly by single-crystal X-ray crystallography, though structures could be solved from PXRD diffraction data in many cases. Matching of the PXRD patterns of the bulk with those simulated from the single-crystal data from separate synthetic routes has also been utilized successfully.
## 7.1 Ligand replacement reactions
Recently metal-carboxylate MOFs have been actively investigated and care must be taken to interpret the IR spectra, especially recorded as KBr pellets, since carboxylate ligands can easily be replaced by bromide ions due to their lability. One such example for ligand exchange is illustrated here where mechanical grinding of KBr with the 1-D ladder CP $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-bpe})_{2}-$ $\left(|\mathsf{u}{-}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{O}_{2}\right)_{2}\left(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{F}_{3}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{O}_{2}\right)_{2}]\left(\bar{\mathbf{5}}\mathbf{8}\right)^{50}$ led to 1-D zigzag CP $[\mathrm{Zn}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{bpe})(\mathrm{Br})_{2}]$ (59). The bridging and chelating ligands acetate and trifluoroacetate were replaced by terminal bromide ligand during mechanochemical grinding of 58 and KBr in $1:2$ ratio.51 Similarly, another 1-D CP $\mathrm{\small{[Cd(CH_{3}C O_{2})_{2}(\upmu\cdot b p e)(H_{2}O)]}}$ $\left(53\right)^{44}$ transformed to 2-D sheet-like CP $\left[\mathrm{Cd}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{bpe})(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathbf{Br})_{2}\right]$ (60) when both the acetate and aqua ligands were replaced by bridging bromide ligands during mechanochemical grinding as shown in Fig. 29.51 Metal replacement reactions and pillar ligand substitutions without changing the overall topology of the MOFs are known in the literature (see Sections 9 and 11). However, in these serendipitous grinding reactions, the ligand substitution changed the structures drastically.
Similarly, ion exchange of thiocyanate bridging ligand by bromide ion has been found responsible for the structural transformation of a 3-D Pb(II) CP to another 3-D CP and this reversible transformation was again investigated by PXRD studies.52
## 7.2 Pedal motions and molecular movements
The $\left[2\substack{+}2\right]$ cycloaddition reaction is a convenient way of probing the reactivity and structural transformation in selected CPs containing photoreactive $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ bonds accelerated by mechanical grinding.
A hydrogen-bonded coordination complex $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{bpe})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]\mathrm{-}$ 一 $\left(\mathbf{NO}_{3}\right)_{2}{\cdot}8/3\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}{\cdot}2/3\mathbf{b}$ pe (61) exhibits such a mechanochemical force induced structural transformation which altered the photoreactivity.53 There are six metal-coordinated bpe molecules arranged in a serial fashion maintaining distances suitable for photodimerization of which two were in parallel and four were in criss-cross orientation. Upon irradiation for $25\mathrm{h}$ , single crystals of this compound produced $39\%$ rctt-tpcb and $7\%$ rtct-tpcb [tpcb $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 1,2,3,4-tetrakis $\mathbf{\bar{4}}^{\prime}$ -pyridyl)cyclobutane] which indicated incomplete pedal-like motion of bpe ligands prior to photodimerization. However, the powdered sample obtained by grinding for $5\mathrm{{min}}$ underwent quantitative conversion having rctt-tpcb as a major product, which indicates that the criss-crossed bpe molecules undergo pedal-like motion and free bpe molecules moved towards each other during grinding and participated in photodimerization reaction. The product of this reaction is expected to generate a 1-D CP as shown in Fig. 30.
## 8. Structural distortion due to pedal-like motion
Various organic molecules containing double bonds with phenyl or pyridyl or other aromatic substituents, e.g. azobenzene, often undergo bicycle pedal-like motion which imposes disorder in crystal structure due to concomitant conformational interconversion.54 Such pedal-like motion was found responsible for various anomalous behaviours such as unexpected photodimerization between criss-cross pairs, photoisomerization, photochromism, etc. An example for pedal motion is illustrated here. The $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ bonds of trans-cinnamamide in its co-crystal with phthalic acid have been found to align in criss-cross arrangement in head-to-head fashion, which underwent pedal-like motion prior to photodimerization, and resulted in the syn-rctt-dimer as shown in Fig. 31.55
In addition to the organic systems and metal complexes,56 CPs were also reported to exhibit pedal-like motion of ligands containing unsaturated bonds. For example, Bharadwaj et al. reported 2-D CP $\begin{array}{r}{[\mathbf{Cd}(\mathbf{azim})_{2}(\mathbf{DMF})_{2}](\mathbf{ClO}_{4})_{2}.}\end{array}$ 2-DMF (62) $\left(\mathsf{a z i m}=\mathsf{\partial}\right.$ bis(4-imidazol-1-yl-phenyl)diazene), which exhibited pedal-like motion of azo groups triggered by both heat and removal of guest molecules57 which can be restored by soaking the single crystals in DMF. The pedal-like motions were also observed by exchanging the solvents with acetone and DEF in SCSC and in reversible manner.
## 9. Structural robustness during metal-ion exchange reactions
In the 3-D network structures, the metal ions and the metal aggregates act as nodes connecting the spacer ligands, thus providing stability to the structures. It may appear that it is difficult to replace these metal ions (nodes) by other metal ions without disrupting the structures including connectivity, crystallinity or even single crystallinity. It is surprising that such metal ion substitutions can be carried out reversibly in SCSC manner in several cases and the details are given below.
Kim et al.58 provided the first example for metal-ion exchange reaction in MOFs. The compound $\mathrm{Cd_{1.5}(H_{3}O)_{3}[(C d_{4}O)_{3}(h e t t)_{8}]\cdot6H_{2}O}$ (63) ( $\mathbf{\dot{H}}_{3}\mathbf{hett}=$ ethyl substituted truxene tricarboxylic acid) having cubic network similar to sodalite structure, exhibited reversible metal-ion exchange in SCSC fashion. Upon immersion of single crystals in an aqueous solution of $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ for a week, all the $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ ions are completely replaced by $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions, maintaining structural integrity and single crystallinity (Fig. 32).
Similar metal-ion exchange in coordination polymers involving a porphyrin moiety has been reported recently by Zaworotko.59 Reaction between biphenyl- $^{3,4^{\prime},5}$ -tricarboxylate $\left(\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{BPT}\right)$ , $\mathbf{CdCl}_{2}$ and meso-tetra(N-methyl-4-pyridyl)porphine tetratosylate (TMPyP) resulted in a CP where cationic CdTMPyP moieties were encapsulated in the anionic framework of $\mathrm{[Cd_{6}(B P T)_{4}C l_{4}(H_{2}O)_{4}]}$ (64). When single crystals of this $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ CP was immersed in methanol solution of $\mathbf{MnCl}_{2}$ , the complete conversion of CdTMPyP to MnTMPyP occurred in one week and the complete exchange of Cd(II) by manganese took place in one month maintaining single crystallinity. Upon metal-ion exchange, the unit cell parameters were reduced because of smaller size of $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ and each manganese ion in MnTMPyP occupies the plane of the porphyrin, in contrast to $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ . The oxidation states of manganese in the porphyrin and frameworks are respectively $+3$ and $^{+2}$ . A number of similar metal ion exchange reactions have been successfully carried out without destroying the framework structures or single crystallinity.60–66
## 10. Structural transformation via ligand addition and elimination reactions
## 10.1 2-D to 3-D structural transformation via ligand insertion
Kitagawa and co-workers introduced a novel concept of inserting bidentate spacer ligands into 2-D CPs to generate pillared-layer 3-D CPs as depicted in Fig. 33.67 For example, the copper paddlewheel SBUs composed of $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{I}}})}$ and tetrafluorobenzene-1,4- dicarboxylate (tfbdc) had two coordinated methanol molecules at the axial positions of the $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{C})_{4}]$ SBU and the 2-D sheets are stacked in a ABAB fashion. The coordinated methanol molecules can be substituted by DABCO as a pillar between two layers, and a three-dimensional CP resulted. Due to ABAB packing of the original 2-D sheets, a lateral movement has been necessary and as a result the single crystallinity has been lost. The final structure of $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(t f b d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]}$ (65) was established by synchrotron X-ray powder diffraction measurements and subsequent Rietveld refinement.
On the contrary, Chen et al. found that the 3-D pillared-layer structure $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]$  $4\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (66) can be transferred to 2-D structure $\big[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}\big]{\cdot}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{F}$ (67) in air for 3 days, in an SCSC manner.68 Apparently water is the driving force for the removal of dabco and hence the structural transformation. Further this process can also be reversed by refluxing the 2-D sheets with dabco in DMF at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 days. Such interesting transformations are yet to be discovered to make this a general phenomenon.
## 11. Structural transformations by ligand replacement/substitution reactions
Similar to the metal-ion substitutions in framework structures, taller pillars in the pillared-layer structures have been substituted by shorter pillars without disturbing the connectivity of the building units. Moreover, the insertion of pillar-like bridging ligands between two bilayers has also been successfully achieved to generate 3-D pillared-bilayer networks from 2-D bilayer networks and is discussed in this section.
11.1 2-D to $\mathbf{2-D^{\prime}}$ , 2-D to 3-D and 3-D to 3-D transformations
Choe et al. discovered 2-D to ${\bf2-D^{\prime}}$ , 2-D to 3-D and 3-D to $3{\cdot}\mathbf{D}^{\prime}$ structural transformations by ligand replacement method in SCSC manner.69 The compound $\mathrm{\small{[Zn(TCPP)(DPNI)_{0.5}(s o l v)_{2}]}}$ (68) (TCPP $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin, $\mathrm{\bfDPNI}=N,N^{\prime}$ -di4-pyridylnaphthalene tetracarboxydiimide) has a 2-D bilayer structure where 2-D paddle-wheel layers of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{2}\mathbf{TCPP}$ are bridged by DPNI pillar ligands.70 The layers are stacked in ABAB fashion with coordinated solvents between the bilayers. When the single crystals were soaked in a solution containing $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine (bpy), the bridging DPNI ligands were replaced by bpy, maintaining single crystallinity, resulting in another 2-D bilayer structure69 which was unattainable as a single phase via a one-step solvothermal synthesis. Although the $P4/n m m$ symmetry was retained, the distance between layers in the bilayer was drastically reduced from 21.2 to $12.8\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and the unit cell volume was also reduced from 8630 to $5793\ \mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ . In the presence of four equivalents of bpy, the coordinated axial solvents were replaced and the 2-D bilayers were further bridged by bpy ligands resulting in a 3-D structure in a non-SCSC fashion as shown in Fig. 34.69,71 During this 2-D to 3-D transformation process, the bilayers needed to shift laterally so that bpy could bind at the axial positions and bridge two $\mathrm{Zn(n)}$ centres. This is reminiscent of the lateral movements of the CPs during the photodimerization reactions discussed for 54 in Section 6.3. Analogous $[\mathrm{Zn(TCPP)(DPNI)}]$ (69) with 3-D structure in which the $\mathrm{Zn_{2}T C P P}$ squares were stacked in ABBA fashion,70 also exhibited similar ligand replacement of DPNI by bpy resulting in another 3-D structure in an SCSC manner maintaining the same symmetry of I4/mmm.71
## 12. Post-synthetic modifications (PSMs) and reactions
Many porous frameworks are stable after removal of the guest molecules and the space inside the pores can be used as a reaction vessel. Since the ligand spacers inside the surface of MOFs are also exposed and accessible, it may be possible to derivatise the preinstalled functional groups attached to these spacers without affecting the overall framework integrity of the structures, as shown in Fig. 35.
Such post-synthetic modification of the MOFs has the advantage of introducing a diverse range of new functional groups in topologically identical MOFs. Using this method, it is possible to fine-tune and optimize the size and shape of the pore surface of MOFs. However, the MOFs should be sufficiently porous to allow the access of the reagents and stable to the reaction conditions and by-products formed. Harsh reagents or reaction conditions can easily destroy the MOF structures. Extensive literature on this subject can be found in many excellent reviews by Cohen $72\substack{-74}$ and hence they are not discussed any further in this review.
## 12.1 Metal-ion uptake in MOFs
A few PSMs involving metal ions are presented in this section, which are not to be confused with the metal-ion substitution reactions discussed in Section 9. Some examples may not be strictly called PSM as one may realize.
Long, Yaghi and co-workers described a CP, Al(OH)(bpydc) (70) $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bpydc}=2,2^{\prime}\right.$ -bipyridine- ${\cdot}5,5^{\prime}{\cdot}$ -dicarboxylic acid), which contains uncoordinated $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine fragments, suitable for post-synthetic uptake of metal ions. Post-synthetic metal insertion in this MOF resulted in $\mathrm{Al(OH)(bpydc)}{\cdot}0.83(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2})$ (71) and $\mathrm{Al(OH)(bpydc)}{\cdot}0.97[\mathrm{Cu(BF_{4})_{2}}]$ (72) which exhibited significant impact on selective gas sorption properties also. Later, Hardie’s group reported a 3-D coordination polymer $[\mathrm{Zn(iso)(L)}]{\cdot}2(\mathrm{DMF})$ (73) (iso $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ isophthalate, $\mathbf{L}=N,N^{\prime}$ -bis(pyridin-4-yl)- $^{\cdot2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine$^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxamide), which exhibits metal uptake and transformed to a new heteronuclear C $\mathbf{P}[\mathbf{ZnCu_{1/3}(i s o)(L)}]\mathbf{Cl_{1/3}}{\cdot}0.3\mathbf{CuCl_{2}}{\cdot}\mathbf{DM}$ F (74) in SCSC fashion. In order for the coordination of $\mathrm{{Cu}(\Omega)}$ to chelate the N donors of $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine fragments, the pyridyl groups needed to rotate. Although the structural change during this transformation (space group changed from $P6_{2}22$ to $P6_{4}22$ ) was small, the solid-state reactivity was noteworthy.76
Derivatisation with metal uptake at the post-synthetically modified ligand sites in MOFs have been observed. Rosseinsky et al. demonstrated that the free $\left.-\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\right.$ group in $[(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O})(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CC}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{3}-$ $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)_{3}]$ (75) could be condensed with salicylaldehyde to obtain a salicylidene moiety which was further used to bind the metal complex $\mathrm{vo}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}$ .77 The uptake of $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ in such post-synthetically modified ligand binding sites was also reported by Yaghi et al.78
The post-synthetically modified demetalation and subsequently a remetalation approach has been demonstrated by Hupp, Nguyen and co-workers on a series of $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{salen})$ based MOFs. The treatment of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ (salen) based MOF with ${\bf H}_{2}{\bf O}_{2}$ resulted in demetalation of the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{3+}$ ions from the salen struts, which could be remetalated with a variety of metal ions to form isostructural metal–organic materials.66
In addition to metal-ion insertion, metal-ion exchange has also been reported in post-synthetic modification. Dong et al. reported a series of lanthanide-based coordination polymers of 3,5-bis(3-carboxyphenyl)-1,2,4-triazol-4-amine (L) containing hydrated $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ cation as guest in a $\mathrm{Ln}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ tetragonal prismatic cage. The encapsulated $\mathrm{[Mn(H_{2}O)_{6}]^{2+}}$ cations were exchanged reversibly with the $\left[\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{8}\right]^{3+}$ cations along with an anion for charge balance, with preservation of the original crystal structure.79 It is worth noting that the smaller cation $\mathrm{\big[Mn\big(H_{2}O\big)_{6}\big]^{2+}}$ was replaced by bigger $\left[\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{8}\right]^{3+}$ cations. Similarly, the exchange of guest $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ cations in $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}[(\mathrm{Mn}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{BTT})_{8}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH})_{10}]_{2}$ (76) $\mathbf{\DeltaBTT}=1,3,5$ -benzenetristetrazolate) with ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ and other bivalent 3d transition-metal cations was also reported to result in the formation of isostructural framework compounds.80
## 13. Dynamic behaviour of coordination polymers
One of the highly studied solid-state structural transformations involving porous CPs is the dynamic behaviour in response to external stimuli. Kitagawa and co-workers in their excellent review classified porous coordination polymers (PCPs) on the basis of their functions into three categories – first, second and third generation, and are summarized in Fig. 36.9
In the first-generation PCPs, the frameworks collapse irreversibly upon removal of the guest molecules from interior core. As a result the crystalline phase will change or may become completely amorphous. Second-generation PCPs possess very stable and robust frameworks and the porosities are accessible reversibly upon repeated guest removal and uptake. This class of materials are analogous to zeolites and can used for gas sorption, storage separation, catalysis and so on. The third-generation PCPs are those which contain soft, flexible and dynamic frameworks which transform reversibly under the influences of external stimuli. In most of the cases, the transformations take place with retention of crystallinity; however, sometimes perfect crystallinity may be lost which can be recovered in a reversible manner. Due to these types of structural flexibility of the highly ordered networks, they are often denoted as soft porous crystals.34 It should be noted that single crystals need not be maintained for the second and third generation PCPs during the guest exchange and structural transformation.
Third-generation PCPs which exhibit reversible expansion and contraction of frameworks in the presence of selective guests that fit the shape, are referred to as breathing frameworks.81 Various types of structural transformations involved in the breathing have been described and are depicted in Fig. 37.82 These enable the soft PCPs to exhibit various properties that are summarized in Fig. 38.34
## 13.1 Removal/exchange of solvents coordinated to metal ions
The removal of the solvents coordinated to metal ions causes changes in the coordination number and geometry, and manifests distortions in their structures. Such processes are expected to influence various properties such as colour, luminescence, magnetism and sorption. The solvochromic (solvent dependent colour change) properties in MOFs have been very well studied for transition-metal ions, especially $\mathrm{{Ni}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ and $\mathrm{Co(\mathrm{n}).}^{\mathrm{85,86}}$ For example, the change in the coordination geometry of $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ from octahedral to squarepyramidal due to removal of an aqua ligand is accompanied by a change in colour from pink to purple for $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ and from green to yellow for $\mathrm{{Ni}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ , as well as changes in their magnetic properties.46
## 13.2 Influence of solvent removal on spin-crossover (SCO) properties
Here how the change in the coordination and local electronic environments due to the loss of guest solvents can dictate the magnetic properties such as spin-crossover behaviour of certain CPs is illustrated. The guest solvent EtOH present in the diagonally interpenetrated rhombic (4,4) grid structure $[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{azpy})_{2}(\mathrm{NCS})_{2}]$ EtOH (77) $\mathsf{\Pi}(\mathsf{a z p y}=4,4^{\prime}$ -azopyridine) was proven to play a crucial role for the observed SCO.87 The EtOH guest molecule was hydrogen bonded to one of the thiocyanate ligands making the two octahedrally coordinated $\mathrm{Fe(n)}$ centers inequivalent. The $\mathrm{Fe(n)}$ core ‘hydrogen-bonded’ to EtOH showed SCO behavior at $150\mathrm{~K~}$ as high spin $(S=2)$ Fe(II) flipped to low spin state $(S=0)$ . Upon removal of the guest, the desolvated compound no longer shows SCO behavior. Due to the removal of EtOH, the two $\mathrm{Fe(n)}$ became equivalent and the space group changed from monoclinic $C2/c$ to orthorhombic Ibam. Further, the angle between the interpenetrating rhombic grids changed from 60.3 to $53.6^{\circ}$ due to a scissor-like movement of the nets. In addition, the rhombic 1-D channels which ran along the crystallographic $c$ -axis, also were deformed and the pore volume decreased from 12 to $2\%$ . Here the SCO property has been influenced by the reversible exchange of the sorbed guest molecules making this an interesting example for molecular sensing.
## 13.3. Effect of soft nature on the gas uptake properties
Soft porous crystals are flexible coordination polymeric network structures which are responsive to guest molecules.34 In this process the conformation of the guest is changed by the external stimuli while the host structure would be transformed akin to the guest shapes. Kitagawa et al. has elegantly demonstrated that such reversible structural transformation is achieved by the light and heat induced cis–trans isomerisation of azobenzene guest molecule in a pillared-layer compound $[\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{2}$ (terephthalate)2(triethylenediamine)] is able to control the nitrogen gas adsorption property.88
Another interesting example was presented by F´erey et al. The framework of MIL-53ht, $\left[\mathrm{Cr(OH)(bdc)}\right]$ (78) $(\mathrm{bdc}~=~1,4-$ benzenedicarboxylate) exhibited framework expansion and contraction triggered by the reorientation of the coordination bonds. MIL-53ht is the high-temperature form which can be obtained by calcination of $\mathrm{\small{[cr(OH)(bdc)](bdc)_{0.75}(H_{2}O)_{\scriptscriptstyle x}}}$ at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and it absorbs moisture at room temperature to yield $\mathrm{[Cr(OH)(bdc)]{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ (79). Upon absorption of water, the pores were observed to shrink as the Cr–O(OH)–Cr angle changed from 128 to $124^{\circ}$ and the channel cross-sections changed from $16.73\times13.04\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ to $19.69\times7.85\mathrm{~\AA~}^{2}$ (Fig. 39). Such breathing behaviour and related sorption properties of analogous MIL-53 compounds containing $\mathrm{\sfAl(\mathrm{_{III}),^{89}\mathrm{~Fe(\mathrm{_{III}),^{90}\mathrm{~In(\mathrm{_{III}),^{91}\mathrm{~Ga(\mathrm{_{III})^{92}}}}}}}}}$ have also been studied in detail.
Kitagawa et al. discovered the flexible nature of the well-known paddle-wheel SBU in the pillared-layer PCP $[\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{2}\mathbf{-}$ $\big(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{bdc}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{d}\mathrm{bp}\mathrm{b}\big)\big]{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{D}\mathbf{M}\mathrm{F}{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ (d $\begin{array}{r l r}{\mathbf{\boldsymbol{b}}{\mathbf{p}}{\mathbf{b}}}&{{}=}&{2{,}3}\end{array}$ -difluoro-1,4- bis(4-pyridyl)benzene) upon evacuating the compound and transforming into tetrahedral coordination geometry through an intermediate state.93 This greatly influences the framework deformation through slippage of layers as well their gas uptake properties.
## 14. Conclusion
In this tutorial review, we have presented an overview of solidstate reactivity and structural transformations of coordination polymers in the solid state involving the formation and breakage of at least a chemical bond. Obviously these are quite different from the phase transitions (also called as phase transformations) observed in inorganic materials. Various ways of carrying out structural transformations (cause) and various types of observed transformations that can happen (effects) are described. CPs exhibit a variety of solid-state reactions that involve changes in coordination spheres by the loss, uptake or exchange of ligands or guest molecules and by external stimuli. The vacant coordination sites at the metal centres created by the removal of ligands are often found to be re-established by the free donor sites of the neighbouring ligands or guest molecules in the solid. Although single-crystal X-ray diffraction is the most used technique to follow the solid-state structural changes, this review reveals that powder diffraction methods are becoming the dominant structural tool for studying these phenomena in the future. In the past, many interesting structural transformations, where single crystallinity was lost, have been overlooked or left unexplored due to the lack of such modern techniques. We anticipate that with the help of modern structure determining tools, many systems undergoing single crystalline-to-polycrystalline and crystalline-to-amorphous-to-crystalline transformations can be routinely studied; and many new discoveries will be made which will establish new milestones on the path of progress of modern solid-state science. The changes in the properties such as chirality, magnetic properties and luminescence by the structural changes can be used in designing advanced functional materials for practical applications such as sensors.
